-- chem/laplace.cub — the two calculus steps of H₂⁺'s chain, as polynomial algebra. (1) The Laplacian in prolate
-- spheroidal coordinates. A function of the distance ρ from the axis and of z is a function g(s, z) of s = ρ², where
-- the Cartesian Laplacian is ∂_ρρ + ρ⁻¹∂_ρ + ∂_zz = 4s∂_ss + 4∂_s + ∂_zz (∂_ρ = 2ρ∂_s). In ξ, η the quantities
-- s = (R²/4)(ξ²−1)(1−η²) and z = (R/2)ξη are polynomials, so for each g the claim
--   4·[∂_ξ(ξ²−1)∂_ξ + ∂_η(1−η²)∂_η] G = R²(ξ²−η²)·(4s g_ss + 4g_s + g_zz)(S, Z),   G = g(S, Z),
-- is an equality of polynomials in ξ and η, checked coefficient by coefficient. Both sides are linear second-order
-- operators in (s, z), fixed by their values on 1, s, z, s², sz, z² (the six coefficient functions are read off them in
-- turn), and each side is a polynomial in R of degree at most 6, checked at R = 1 … 7; so ∇² = 4/(R²(ξ²−η²))·[∂_ξ(ξ²−1)∂_ξ
-- + ∂_η(1−η²)∂_η] on every function of ρ and z (`lap-grid`), with no metric and no square root. (2) The Legendre
-- equation d/dη[(1−η²)P_l′] = −l(l+1)P_l for the polynomials of Bonnet's recurrence, every l ≤ 24 (`leg-ode`).
-- One polynomial type serves both variables: a polynomial over a ring given by its operations, and a polynomial in ξ and
-- η is a polynomial in ξ over polynomials in η.
import "separate.cub"

